{"name":"Eosin Pink","hex":"#ff5ec4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"eosin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A red, acidic dye commonly used in histological stains."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eosin"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eosin","blocks":[{"text":"Eosin is the name of several fluorescent acidic compounds which bind to and form salts with basic, or eosinophilic, compounds like proteins containing basic amino acid residues such as histidine, arginine and lysine, and stains them dark red or pink as a result of the actions of bromine on eosin. In addition to staining proteins in the cytoplasm, it can be used to stain collagen and muscle fibers for examination under the microscope. Structures that stain readily with eosin are termed eosinophilic. In the field of histology, Eosin Y is the form of eosin used most often as a histologic stain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Eosin_Y_Structural_Formulae_V.1.svg/330px-Eosin_Y_Structural_Formulae_V.1.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eosin","wikidata":"Q422637"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Eosin_solution.jpg/500px-Eosin_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Eosin_solution.jpg/1280px-Eosin_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Eosin_solution.jpg/500px-Eosin_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eosin Y solution for staining microscopy slides","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEosin_solution.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Eosinophilic%2C_basophilic%2C_chromophobic_and_amphophilic_staining.png/500px-Eosinophilic%2C_basophilic%2C_chromophobic_and_amphophilic_staining.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Eosinophilic%2C_basophilic%2C_chromophobic_and_amphophilic_staining.png/500px-Eosinophilic%2C_basophilic%2C_chromophobic_and_amphophilic_staining.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eosinophilic staining, compared to other patterns when using hematoxylin and eosin (H&E)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEosinophilic%2C_basophilic%2C_chromophobic_and_amphophilic_staining.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Veld_met_irissen_bij_Arles_-_s0037V1962_-_Van_Gogh_Museum.jpg/500px-Veld_met_irissen_bij_Arles_-_s0037V1962_-_Van_Gogh_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Veld_met_irissen_bij_Arles_-_s0037V1962_-_Van_Gogh_Museum.jpg/1280px-Veld_met_irissen_bij_Arles_-_s0037V